⏺ Here's how it works when you buy from the Indiana dealer and register in
  Dallas. Good news: you won't be double-taxed, and the rates happen to line up
  cleanly.

  Sales tax: pay 6.25% at the dealer, owe ~$0 more in Texas

  At Napleton (Indiana side): Indiana law has a special rule for exactly your
  situation. If the vehicle will be moved and titled in another state within 30
  days of sale, the Indiana dealer collects sales tax at your destination 
  state's rate — Texas's 6.25% — instead of Indiana's 7%. The dealer documents
  this on Form ST-108NR (nonresident) and remits it to Indiana. On $41,497
  that's about $2,594, paid at signing. (The "nonreciprocal state" list you may
  see referenced — Arizona, California, Florida, etc. — only applies to RVs and
  cargo trailers, not passenger cars.)

  At the Dallas County tax office (Texas side): Texas residents bringing in a
  vehicle bought out of state owe 6.25% motor vehicle use tax on the sales
  price, less credit for sales tax legally paid to another state. Since Indiana
  collected exactly 6.25%, the credit zeroes it out — you pay no additional tax,
  just title and registration fees.

  Two things to nail down with the dealer before signing: confirm they'll
  process it as an ST-108NR nonresident sale at the Texas rate (not Indiana's
  7%), and get a copy of the ST-108NR plus the purchase order showing tax
  collected — that's your proof for the credit in Dallas.

  Documentation and steps

  Leave the dealership with:
  1. Form ST-108NR + purchase agreement showing price and tax paid
  2. Properly assigned Indiana title (with odometer disclosure) — or
  confirmation of where/when they'll mail it, especially if you finance
  3. Temporary/interim plate from the dealer for the drive home
  4. Form 130-U (Application for Texas Title and/or Registration) — bigger
  dealer groups like Napleton often fill this out for out-of-state buyers; ask
  if they'll handle Texas titling through a title service

  Before you drive: add the car (by VIN) to your Texas auto insurance policy —
  you'll need proof of Texas liability coverage to register.

  Once in Dallas, within 30 days of the sale date:
  1. Get an emissions inspection. Dallas County is one of Texas's 17 emissions
  counties — the safety inspection was eliminated in 2025, but a passing
  emissions test is still required for registration. Any Texas inspection
  station, ~$25–40.
  2. Go to the Dallas County Tax Assessor-Collector with: Form 130-U, the
  assigned title, proof of tax paid (ST-108NR), proof of insurance, and your ID.
  3. Pay the fees: ~$33 title application, ~$51 base registration plus small
  local fees, and a $7.50 inspection-replacement fee — roughly $100–150 all-in.

  Miss the 30-day window and Texas adds a 5% penalty on the tax (10% after 60
  days) — and the 30-day deadline also underpins Indiana's lower-rate treatment,
  so don't let the paperwork sit.

  Bottom line: total tax+registration cost is ~$2,700 whether you buy in
  Indianapolis or Dallas — the tax is a complete wash, so the ~$2,200–8,400
  price advantage on the Napleton car survives intact.

